  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "If the two solutions of the equation $x^2-5x+c=0$ differ by 1, then find the value of $c$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$c=6$.",
      "worked": "Let the smaller solution be $r$, so the other is $r+1$. Expanding the corresponding factors gives\n$$\\begin{align*}(x-r)(x-r-1)&=0\\\\x^2-(2r+1)x+r(r+1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nComparing the coefficient of $x$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2r+1&=5\\\\2r&=4\\\\r&=2\\end{align*}$$\nHence the solutions are 2 and 3, and $c=2\\times3=6$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 76
  },
